    <!-- 5.0: the About screen. -->
    <string name="about_version">Version %1$s</string>
    <string name="about_qubic_title">Qubic</string>
    <string name="about_qubic">Qubic is tic-tac-toe in three dimensions: a cube of 4 × 4 × 4 cells, that is 64 cells and 76 possible lines. The first player to line up four pieces of their color wins. Sold as a board game in the 1960s, it was solved in 1980 by Oren Patashnik: the first player can always force a win.</string>
    <string name="about_tetrano_title">Tetrano</string>
    <string name="about_tetrano">Tetrano is the two-dimensional representation of Qubic invented by Maxime Vial in 2004, which this app uses: the four levels of the cube become four sizes of rings, placed on a 4 × 4 board. A line then reads as four concentric rings in one square, or as four rings in a row, all the same size or in increasing or decreasing size.</string>
    <string name="about_app_title">The app</string>
    <string name="about_app">This app was written by Thibaut PERRIN. Started in 2014, it was brought up to date in 2026 with the help of artificial intelligence: a complete rewrite, a new computer player based on a neural network, puzzles and game analysis.</string>
    <string name="about_open_source_title">Open source</string>
    <string name="about_open_source">The source code of Tetrano is open source, under the MIT license, and available on GitHub.</string>
    <string name="about_github">See the code on GitHub</string>
